Split out of #1189 at the maintainer's request. Refs #1188 (the panic half); the abort half is #1189.

Under `sync`, `Dynamic::is_locked` returns `false` for every shared value, on the reasoning that access waits for the `RwLock`. Under `not(unchecked)` it does not wait indefinitely: `locked_read` tries a few times and gives up, and `type_id()` / `type_name()` then unwrap the `None` and panic. That always happens when the current thread itself holds the value locked for write:

```rust
#[derive(Clone)]
struct Thing(i64);

fn with_arg(ctx: NativeCallContext, t: Thing, f: FnPtr) -> Result<Dynamic, Box<EvalAltResult>> {
    f.call_within_context(&ctx, (t,))
}

let mut e = Engine::new();
e.register_type_with_name::<Thing>("Thing");
e.register_fn("with_arg", with_arg);
e.register_fn("get", |t: &mut Thing| t.0);
let mut scope = Scope::new();
scope.push("t", Thing(7));
// `||` takes no argument but `with_arg` passes one; `t` is captured, so the method call holds it locked.
e.eval_with_scope::<Dynamic>(&mut scope, "t.with_arg(|| t.get())");
```

Without `sync`, #1161's `ensure_no_data_race` turns this into `ErrorDataRace`. Under `sync` it does not, because `is_locked` says the value is free; the call goes on to `resolve_fn`, which hashes each argument's `type_id()`, and that read gives up and panics.

The change makes `is_locked` under `sync` (and not `unchecked`) return `true` exactly when `locked_read` would give up, so the existing data-race check covers `sync` as well. Under `sync` + `unchecked` access blocks rather than gives up, so `is_locked` stays `false`.

**This changes the documented meaning of a public method under `sync`** (the doc said shared values "are never locked" there), which is why it is its own PR for you to shape. Another option is to keep `is_locked` as it is and make `ensure_no_data_race` probe `locked_read` itself.

Test: `test_closures_capturing_locked_method_receiver` in `tests/closures.rs`, gated on `no_closure` (the closure must capture the variable to share it; that was the CI failure on the `no_closure` row), `no_object` (the method-call syntax) and `unchecked`. Under `sync` without this change it panics in `type_id`; with it, `ErrorDataRace` under both feature sets.

Checks: `cargo test --features sync`, `cargo test --test closures` with default features and under the `no_closure` CI row, `cargo +nightly fmt --check`. CHANGELOG line under 1.27.0's bug fixes, linking this PR.

